(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-atherosclerosis-lipids-vignettes"]={"title":"Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 17-year-old girl began isotretinoin for severe acne three months ago. Her triglyceride has risen from 120 to 410 mg/dL, with normal thyroid and kidney function. Which is the most likely cause of the rise?","choices":["Anabolic steroids","Hypercholesterolemia","Isotretinoin","Familial chylomicronemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Isotretinoin, used for acne, can elevate triglyceride, as can estrogen, glucocorticoids, beta blockers and bexarotene. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a prior heart attack asks whether eating fish will help his heart. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Fish can replace his statin","Eat fish only once a month","Eat fish at least twice a week","Fish raises his coronary risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fatty fish or fish-oil supplements are associated with lower risk of coronary disease and coronary death, and at least 2 servings of fish per week are recommended. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman who smokes a pack a day has a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 13% and an uncertain statin decision. Her coronary artery calcium score is zero. Which conclusion is inappropriate?","choices":["Advising her to stop smoking","Counting smoking as a risk factor","Withholding a statin because of the zero","Continuing the statin discussion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A zero score lowers risk and can justify withholding or delaying a statin, except in cigarette smokers, diabetes and strong family history; she smokes, so the zero does not settle it. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man meets four criteria for metabolic syndrome, including a waist circumference of 43 inches. He asks what the main treatment is. Which answer is most appropriate?","choices":["Lifestyle therapy","A fibrate first","Apheresis sessions","Statin alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In all age groups, lifestyle therapy is the primary intervention for metabolic syndrome, including weight reduction of 5% to 10% and daily moderate activity. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has low-density lipoprotein of 290 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride. She has firm nodules along both Achilles tendons (tendon xanthomas), and her father had a heart attack at 48. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Mixed dyslipidemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein of 200 to 400 mg/dL, normal triglyceride, Achilles tendon xanthomas (present in about 75%) and premature coronary disease in a first-degree relative fit one mutant allele. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with a family history of premature heart disease had total and high-density cholesterol drawn without fasting at a health fair. What do these two values allow?","choices":["Diagnosis of familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Confirmation of homozygous familial disease","Calculation of her total-to-high-density ratio","Calculation of her low-density lipoprotein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Total and high-density cholesterol need no fasting, give the total-to-high-density ratio (the most efficient and cost-effective way to predict coronary risk) and can be used for risk assessment. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has low-density lipoprotein of 175 mg/dL. His father had a heart attack at 50, and he has no other risk factors. When should a statin be considered for him?","choices":["Only if xanthomas appear","Never, as risk scores do not apply","Now, given family history and his level","Only once he reaches age 40"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. At 20 to 39, lifetime risk is estimated to encourage lifestyle, and a statin is considered with a family history of premature disease and low-density lipoprotein of 160 or more. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with hypertension was switched to a beta blocker three months ago. His triglyceride has risen from 140 to 260 mg/dL, and his thyroid and kidney tests are normal. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["The beta blocker can raise triglyceride","The beta blocker lowers triglyceride","The rise must be familial disease","The rise means he has hypothyroidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers can elevate triglyceride, as can estrogen, glucocorticoids, isotretinoin and bexarotene, so a new rise after starting one should be recognized as a secondary cause. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has low-density lipoprotein of 260 mg/dL, normal triglyceride and firm nodules over the extensor tendons of his hands (tendon xanthomas). His brother had a heart attack at 44. Which drug regimen is started first?","choices":["Evolocumab alone","High-intensity statin","Low-intensity statin","Fibrate therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The common regimen in heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia is a high-intensity statin, with ezetimibe and nicotinic acid added if needed and aggressive reduction of other vascular risk factors. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man is starting atorvastatin after a heart attack. He asks what blood tests he will need while taking it. Which answer is most appropriate?","choices":["No monitoring is required at all","Lipids, liver function and creatine kinase","Thyroid function and a full blood count","Kidney function and potassium levels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Statin monitoring covers lipid levels, liver function and creatine kinase (creatine phosphokinase), so he can expect all three to be checked. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with a prior ischemic stroke has taken a high-intensity statin for years without problems. She asks whether she should stop it now that she is over 75. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Only lifestyle is advised now","Continuing it is reasonable","Statins are stopped at 75","Switch to a fibrate instead"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Above 75 with clinical atherosclerotic disease, continuing a high-intensity statin is reasonable, as is starting a moderate- or high-intensity statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with a baseline low-density lipoprotein of 205 mg/dL has taken a high-intensity statin reliably for three months. His low-density lipoprotein is now 122 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add gemfibrozil","Stop the statin","Change nothing","Add ezetimibe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In severe primary hypercholesterolemia, if low-density lipoprotein remains 100 or more on a high-intensity statin, adding ezetimibe is reasonable. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with two prior heart attacks, diabetes and hypertension takes a maximal statin. Her low-density lipoprotein is 95 mg/dL, and she takes no other lipid drug. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add ezetimibe","Add evolocumab","Add niacin","Stop the statin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In very high-risk disease, when low-density lipoprotein stays 70 or more on a maximally tolerated statin, adding ezetimibe is reasonable, and it comes before any injectable. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman on a statin has reached her low-density lipoprotein goal, but her triglyceride remains 260 mg/dL after lifestyle changes. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add a fibrate or niacin","Stop the statin and watch","Continue with no change","Begin apheresis sessions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The primary aim is the low-density goal, and if triglyceride stays above 200 mg/dL once it is reached, additional triglyceride-lowering therapy (a fibrate or niacin) is indicated. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has yellow deposits in the creases of his palms (palmar xanthomas). His triglyceride is 410 mg/dL and total cholesterol 390 mg/dL. Genetic testing shows homozygosity for apolipoprotein E2. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Metabolic syndrome","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An apolipoprotein E mutation with apolipoprotein E2 homozygosity lets chylomicron and very-low-density remnants accumulate, raising triglyceride and total cholesterol both to 250 to 500, with distinctive palmar xanthomas. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia smokes, has hypertension and eats fast food daily. He starts a high-intensity statin. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["His disorder is cured once levels fall","Stop smoking and cut saturated fat","The statin makes diet changes unneeded","Smoking matters only for lung disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment includes aggressive reduction of associated vascular risk factors (smoking, hypertension, diabetes) and a diet low in saturated fat and cholesterol, alongside the statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man without diabetes has a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 9%. Because the statin decision was uncertain, a coronary artery calcium score was obtained, and it is 180. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Start statin therapy","Withhold the statin","Repeat the score yearly","Start a fibrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A calcium score of 100 or more (or at or above the 75th percentile) indicates statin therapy unless the clinician-patient risk discussion defers it. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with a sedentary job has a triglyceride of 280 mg/dL, low-density lipoprotein of 165 mg/dL and non-high-density cholesterol of 205 mg/dL. He has no xanthomas. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mixed dyslipidemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 150 with low-density lipoprotein above 130 or non-high-density above 160 defines mixed dyslipidemia, the most common type in clinical practice. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, hypertension and current smoking has low-density lipoprotein of 135 mg/dL. He has no atherosclerotic disease. Which statin intensity is reasonable for him?","choices":["No statin, lifestyle only","Statin only after a stroke","High-intensity statin","Low-intensity statin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In diabetes at higher risk, especially with multiple risk factors or age 50 to 75, a high-intensity statin to lower low-density lipoprotein by 50% or more is reasonable. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has soft yellow plaques on both upper eyelids near the inner canthi (xanthelasmas). Her low-density lipoprotein is 205 mg/dL. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["They are caused by her eye makeup","They suggest an inherited lipid disorder","They are harmless and need no work-up","They show her triglyceride is very high"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Xanthelasmas usually indicate an underlying genetic condition, and ruling out primary causes matters because they influence her own risk and the management of her family members. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman without diabetes has a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 6% and low-density lipoprotein of 150 mg/dL. She went through menopause at 38. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order a skin biopsy","Discuss a moderate-intensity statin","Start a high-intensity statin","No discussion is needed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At borderline risk (5% to under 7.5%), risk enhancers such as premature menopause before 40 prompt a risk discussion about moderate-intensity statin therapy. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman had a routine lipid panel drawn two hours after breakfast, and the order included triglyceride and a calculated low-density lipoprotein. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Accept the non-fasting values","Start a statin on these values","Order a calcium score instead","Repeat after a 9- to 12-hour fast"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride and a calculated low-density lipoprotein need a 9- to 12-hour fast; only total and high-density cholesterol are valid without fasting. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has severe epigastric pain radiating to his back. Clusters of small yellowish-white papules (eruptive xanthomas) cover his buttocks and the backs of his arms, and fundoscopy shows opalescent retinal vessels (lipemia retinalis). His fasting triglyceride is 2100 mg/dL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Metabolic syndrome","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fasting triglyceride above 500 and usually above 1000 mg/dL, acute pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas and lipemia retinalis (sometimes with hepatosplenomegaly) define this syndrome, which can present in childhood or adulthood. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a triglyceride of 350 mg/dL. Her clinician wants the lipid measure that best reflects her heart-disease risk. Which measure should be used?","choices":["Non-high-density cholesterol","High-density cholesterol only","Total cholesterol only","Triglyceride alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In hypertriglyceridemia, non-high-density cholesterol (all cholesterol except the high-density fraction) gives a better assessment of heart-disease risk than low-density lipoprotein alone. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 14% is considering a statin for primary prevention. Which step should come before the prescription?","choices":["A clinician-patient risk discussion","A skin biopsy for receptor activity","A trial of a fibrate for three months","Liver biopsy to prove statin safety"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adults 40 to 75 in primary prevention have a risk discussion first: risk factors, 10-year risk, enhancers, benefits of lifestyle and statins, adverse effects and interactions, cost, and his preferences. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl has total cholesterol of 690 mg/dL, normal triglyceride and xanthomas on her wrists and knees. Both parents have high cholesterol. Which test confirms the suspected diagnosis?","choices":["Repeat non-fasting total cholesterol","Skin biopsy with receptor activity","Coronary artery calcium score","Fundoscopy for retinal opalescence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia is confirmed by a skin biopsy with measurement of low-density lipoprotein receptor activity; the heterozygous form has no definitive test. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has clusters of small yellowish-white papules (eruptive xanthomas) on his elbows after a weekend of heavy drinking. His triglyceride is 900 mg/dL and his low-density lipoprotein is normal. Which lipoproteins are most likely extremely elevated?","choices":["High-density lipoprotein and apolipoprotein A-I","Intermediate-density lipoprotein alone","Chylomicrons or very-low-density lipoprotein","Low-density lipoprotein and lipoprotein(a)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Eruptive xanthomas are usually seen with extremely high chylomicrons or very-low-density lipoprotein, and alcohol inhibits hepatic fatty-acid oxidation, raising triglyceride. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man had a heart attack two years ago and takes no lipid-lowering drug. His low-density lipoprotein is 140 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate statin therapy?","choices":["Low-intensity statin","Moderate-intensity statin","Risk calculation first","High-intensity statin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clinical atherosclerotic disease at 75 or younger calls for a high-intensity statin to lower low-density lipoprotein by 50% or more; moderate intensity only if high intensity is not tolerated. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with borderline-high low-density lipoprotein asks which foods can help lower his cholesterol. Which dietary advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Add more saturated and trans fat","Eat more refined carbohydrate","Cut out all vegetables","Add soluble fiber and plant sterols"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Therapeutic lifestyle changes include less saturated fat, more soluble fiber and plant stanols or sterols, cholesterol-lowering substances found in grains, vegetables, fruits, nuts and seeds. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a high-density lipoprotein of 38 mg/dL wants an exercise plan to raise it. Which plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Heavy weight lifting once a week","Gentle stretching on weekends only","Complete rest until levels improve","Brisk exercise 30 minutes, 5 days a week"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Frequent aerobic exercise raises high-density lipoprotein about 5%, meaning brisk exercise 30 minutes a day, 5 days a week. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 112"}]};
